MODNAS is a solid contribution with genuine novelty in cross-device multi-objective NAS. The MetaHypernetwork design combining device embeddings with preference vectors is clean and practical. The NB201 experiments with ground-truth verification across 19 devices are strong. The main weaknesses are: (1) the "first" claim is overreaching, (2) linear scalarization has theoretical limitations for non-convex fronts (acknowledged experimentally but not discussed as a limitation), (3) supernet-only evaluation for 3/4 search spaces, and (4) some evaluation asymmetry for baselines on large-scale spaces. These are real but not fatal — the method clearly works well empirically and the efficiency gains are substantial.

