### Harsh Critic
# Review: Causal Graph Learning via Distributional Invariance of Cause-Effect Relationship

## Critical Issues

### 1. Structural: The invariance test cannot uniquely identify parent sets

The central theoretical claim — that the invariance of P(X|Z) to changes in source priors provides a test for Z = Pa[X] — is incomplete in a way that invalidates the selection mechanism in Eq. (3). Theorem 1 correctly establishes V[P₊(X|Z)] > 0 ⇒ Z ≠ Pa[X]. However, V[P₊(X|Z)] = 0 does **not** imply Z = Pa[X], even in the limit m → ∞. By the local Markov property (which the paper itself states as Eq. (II)), X ⊥ (non-descendants of X \ Pa[X]) | Pa[X]. Therefore, for any superset Z ⊇ Pa[X] where the additional variables are non-descendants d-separated from X given Pa[X], we have P(X|Z) = P(X|Pa[X]), and the variance is also zero. A concrete example: if Pa[X] = {A} and S is a co-parent (spouse) of X (i.e., X → C ← S exists), then S is in M(X), and P(X|{A,S}) = P(X|{A}) since X ⊥ S | {A}. The invariance test cannot distinguish Z = {A,S} from Z = {A}.

The paper explicitly claims the opposite in Section 4.1: *"When m is infinitely large, the implication in Eq. (1) becomes bi-directional and V[P₊(X|Z)] = 0 definitively implies Z = Pa[X]."* This statement is false. Since the maximal cliques in G'(X) (Section 4.3) can contain Pa[X] plus such non-descendant variables, the minimum-variance selection in Eq. (3) can and will select supersets of Pa[X], producing spurious edges. The paper provides no mechanism to identify the minimal parent set among low-variance candidates.

### 2. Structural: Basis variables are not source variables — the theoretical guarantee does not cover the implementation

Theorem 1's guarantee applies when P(Sources) is varied. The implementation (Section 4.2) instead varies P(Basis), where the basis is defined as a set of mutually independent variables (Definition 3). While basis variables share some properties with sources (Theorem 2: equal cardinality), they are not the same set. When Basis ≠ Sources, the resampling procedure in Section 4.2.2 produces D_i ∼ P_i(Basis) · P(X\Basis | Basis), which is **not** the same as the interventional distribution P_int(Sources) · P(X\Sources | Sources) assumed by Theorem 1.

Consequences: source variables that are not in the basis might not have their priors adequately varied, meaning variables downstream of those omitted sources could have their P(X|Z) remain approximately unchanged across augmentations — even when Z ≠ Pa[X]. This creates a risk of false invariance detection (missing edges) that the paper's theory does not account for. Section 4.2.1 offers an informal argument about "shared dependence sets" but no formal guarantee that the invariance test's properties transfer from sources to basis variables.

### 3. Evidential: O(d²) complexity claim rests on unsupported sparsity assumptions

The O(d²) complexity claim requires the degeneracy p of the augmented graph G'(X) (Definition 4) to be a small constant. While p ≤ 13 is reported in experiments (Section 4.3), all experimental graphs are very sparse (E = V in the normal setting, E ≤ 2V in the extreme setting). For graphs with many v-structures — which produce large Markov blankets and thus dense augmented graphs — p grows with d. The Bron-Kerbosch complexity is O(d·p·3^{p/3}), which becomes exponential when p = O(d). The paper does not bound p theoretically, making the O(d²) claim unsubstantiated for the general case. The empirical bounds on p are not sufficient evidence because they are measured only on graphs much sparser than many real-world causal networks.

### 4. Methodological gap: P(X|Z) estimation and the variance metric are unspecified

Eq. (2) and (3) compute ‖P_i(X|Z) − P̄(X|Z)‖², but the paper never specifies: (a) how P(X|Z) is estimated from data (parametric? kernel density? histogram?), (b) what norm ‖·‖ is used (L²? total variation?), or (c) how the norm handles the continuous case after binning (Section 4.2.2 mentions binning but gives no bin width selection criterion). These choices directly determine the sensitivity and specificity of the invariance test. Without specifying them, the method is not reproducible and there is no way to assess whether the empirical results depend critically on particular estimator choices.

## Section-by-Section Notes

**Abstract and Introduction.** The abstract claims "up to 25x reduction in processing time" and "73.3% reduction in spurious rate." The 25x figure is cherry-picked from comparison against NOTEARS on specific synthetic settings; against GIES on real-world data (Table 2, Munin), GLIDE is ~100× slower (6200s vs 61.5s). The 73.3% figure appears to be the maximum reduction against the worst baseline in a specific setting, not a representative average. These claims misrepresent the actual comparison landscape.

**Section 3 (Problem Formulation).** The causal sufficiency assumption (no confounders) is mentioned but its implications for the method are not analyzed. The invariance test relies on there being no hidden confounders among the source variables, but this is not discussed.

**Section 4.1 (Overview).** The bidirectional implication claim discussed in Critical Issue 1 appears here. Paragraph 3 after Eq. (2) makes the incorrect claim about bidirectionality in the infinite-m limit.

**Section 4.2.1 (Finding Source Variables).** The basis-finding algorithm (Theorem 3) relies on pairwise independence tests, whose errors propagate through the entire pipeline. A false independence (failing to detect dependence) could place a non-source into the basis, corrupting the resampling procedure. No sensitivity analysis is provided.

**Section 4.2.2 (Resampling).** Theorem 4 and 5 are correct but rely on categorical (binned) data. For continuous data, the binning resolution directly affects the minimum downsampling rate γ_i and thus the quality of the augmented datasets. No analysis of bin width impact is provided.

**Section 4.3 (Finding Plausible Parent Sets).** Theorem 7 correctly states Pa[X] is a clique, but the subsequent restriction to maximal cliques creates the problem described in Critical Issue 1. The text claims p ≤ 13 "across all benchmark datasets even for the largest graphs," but the largest synthetic graphs have 500 nodes and only 1000 edges — still extremely sparse.

**Section 5 (Experiments).** Table 1 notes that most baselines are not applicable to categorical data, but no justification is given for why NOTEARS variants (which have discrete extensions) and SCORE/DAS are excluded. On continuous data, the comparison is more comprehensive, but the "extreme" setting (E ≤ 2V) still tests only sparse regimes where GLIDE's sparsity assumption holds. The real-world datasets (Table 2) are almost all small; only Munin (1041 nodes) is large, and there GLIDE's runtime is 6200s versus GIES's 61.5s — a 100× slowdown not acknowledged in the text. No statistical significance tests are reported despite 10 runs.

## Strengths

1. The core insight — leveraging the invariance of P(X|Pa[X]) to changes in P(Sources) for causal discovery — is a genuinely interesting and under-explored perspective that differs meaningfully from standard constraint- and score-based approaches.
2. The framework integrates several components (basis identification, principled resampling with minimum downsampling rate, convex hull construction for source priors) in a coherent pipeline, and Theorems 4–6 on the resampling geometry are correct and potentially useful beyond this specific application.
3. The method handles both continuous and categorical data without imposing parametric assumptions on the causal mechanism, which is a real advantage over model-based methods.
4. The experimental evaluation is extensive in terms of the number of baselines and datasets tested, and the code is publicly available.

## Missing Parts and Places to Improve

### Missing Experiments
1. **Dense graph experiments**: Test GLIDE on graphs where E ≥ 3V or 5V to evaluate whether the sparsity-based complexity and accuracy claims hold when p grows. Without this, the scalability claims are only validated for a narrow regime.
2. **Sensitivity to Markov blanket errors**: Run experiments where the Markov blanket identification step is perturbed (e.g., by varying the CI test threshold) to measure how errors propagate through the parent-finding pipeline.
3. **Comparison of maximum clique vs. true Pa[X] size**: Report how often Pa[X] is a proper subset of the selected maximal clique, to directly measure the severity of Critical Issue 1.

### Deeper Analysis Needed
1. **Formal analysis of the supersets-with-zero-variance problem**: Characterize the conditions under which the invariance test is insufficient to identify Pa[X], and propose a resolution (e.g., a minimality criterion, or combining with conditional independence tests to prune supersets). Without this, the theoretical foundation is incomplete.
2. **Formal analysis of the basis-vs-source gap**: Prove or disprove that changing P(Basis) induces sufficient variation in P(X|Z) for Z ≠ Pa[X] to be detected. At minimum, provide conditions under which the test remains sound when using basis surrogates.

### Visualizations & Case Studies
1. **Per-variable parent set analysis**: Show specific cases where GLIDE's selected maximal clique differs from the true Pa[X], to reveal whether the superset issue (Critical Issue 1) causes systematic edge direction errors at v-structures.

### Obvious Next Steps
1. **Add a minimality selection step**: After finding the maximum clique with minimum variance, search its subsets for a minimal set with comparable variance, to mitigate the superset problem.

## Overall Assessment

This paper introduces a creative and conceptually appealing approach to causal discovery based on distributional invariance under source prior shifts. However, it contains two structural flaws that undermine its core claims. First, the invariance test cannot uniquely identify parent sets: any superset of Pa[X] that adds non-descendants d-separated from X given Pa[X] will also have zero variance, and the paper incorrectly asserts bidirectionality in the infinite-m limit. Second, there is an unbridged gap between the theoretical guarantee (which applies to source variables) and the implementation (which uses basis variables as surrogates), with no formal argument that the test remains valid. These are not fixable by adding experiments — they require either revising the theoretical claims or introducing additional mechanisms (e.g., a minimality criterion) that the current work lacks. The empirical results may look favorable in practice due to estimation noise acting as an implicit regularizer and the sparsity of tested graphs, but the theoretical foundation as stated does not support the paper's central claims. The paper should not be accepted in its current form.
### Strength Finder
## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)

1. **Principled invariance test without functional-form assumptions**: Theorem 1 (Section 4.1) establishes that if $\mathbb{V}[P_+(X \mid Z)] > 0$ then $Z \neq \text{Pa}[X]$, providing a test for causal parentage that does not assume linearity or Gaussian noise—unlike NOTEARS, SCORE, or DAS. This is the theoretical backbone that enables the method's model-agnostic applicability verified across all data models in Table 1.

2. **Theoretically grounded data augmentation pipeline**: Theorems 4, 5, and 6 (Section 4.2) jointly provide a complete, principled procedure for generating augmented datasets: Theorem 4 gives the minimum downsampling rate, Theorem 5 gives the optimal resampling procedure achieving that rate, and Theorem 6 characterizes the convex hull of valid source priors satisfying a minimum downsampling threshold $\gamma_0$. This avoids ad hoc augmentation choices and ensures the invariance test is both valid and informative.

3. **Quadratic-complexity parent search via maximal cliques**: Theorem 7 (Section 4.3) establishes that candidate parent sets correspond to cliques in an augmented bidirectional graph built from Markov blankets, reducing the search from exponential to $O(dp3^{p/3})$. The empirical finding that degeneracy $p \leq 13$ across all benchmarks (Section 4.3) makes this effectively $O(d)$ per variable, yielding total $O(d^2)$ complexity—a major improvement over brute-force enumeration and a key enabler of the claimed 25× speedup.

4. **Strong empirical performance on spurious rate**: On the largest real-world dataset (Munin, 1041 variables), GLIDE achieves a spurious rate of 1.8% versus GIES's 42.4% (Table 2), demonstrating that the distributional invariance test yields substantially more reliable causal predictions than score-based methods. On synthetic continuous data with 500 nodes, GLIDE achieves 4.2% spurious rate versus NOTEARS's 11.75% and MLP-NOTEARS's 13.19% (Section 5.1).

### Supporting strengths

1. **Model-agnostic breadth**: GLIDE is the only method in Table 1 applicable across all four data model categories (L-G, nL-nG, categorical synthetic, categorical real-world), while model-based methods (NOTEARS, DAS, SCORE) fail on categorical data and constraint-based methods (FCI) are too slow for large graphs.

2. **Scalability demonstrated on large graphs**: Where PC fails to return results within 48 hours on Pathfinder (86 nodes) and Munin (1041 nodes), marked "N/A" in Table 2, GLIDE successfully processes Munin in ~6200 seconds. On synthetic continuous data with 500 nodes, GLIDE runs 96.54× and 15.66× faster than NOTEARS and MLP-NOTEARS respectively (Section 5.1).

3. **Basis set as surrogate for source variables**: The use of a maximum basis set (Definition 3, Theorems 2–3) as a practical substitute for the true set of source variables is a clever workaround that avoids needing to identify sources (which requires intervention), while Theorem 2 guarantees the basis has the same cardinality as the source set.

### Presentation / practical strengths

1. **Clear algorithmic workflow**: Figure 1 cleanly decomposes the framework into two parts (invariance test preparation and parent-set finding), making the method easy to follow despite the theoretical depth.

2. **Public code availability**: The paper provides a public anonymous code repository, supporting reproducibility.

## Summary

The paper introduces GLIDE, a causal discovery framework that exploits the invariance of $P(\text{effect} \mid \text{cause})$ to changes in $P(\text{cause})$ to identify causal parent sets without assuming a specific data generation model. The single most important piece of evidence delivering on this contribution is the broad empirical evaluation (Figures 2–3, 4, Table 2), where GLIDE achieves the best SHD across all 7 real-world datasets, dramatically lower spurious rates than score-based methods (e.g., 1.8% vs. 42.4% on Munin), and up to 25× speedup over model-based baselines—all enabled by the theoretically grounded invariance test (Theorem 1), principled augmentation pipeline (Theorems 4–6), and quadratic-complexity parent search via maximal cliques (Theorem 7).

