## Section-by-Section Notes

**Abstract & Introduction:**
- The claim that two-stage models are "inefficient in 3D key point detection" is vague — inefficient in what sense? Computational cost? Accuracy? The paper provides no efficiency comparison (FLOPs, params, inference time) between SoloPose and any baseline.
- The many-to-many advantage is claimed but never experimentally validated. No comparison with a many-to-one variant of SoloPose is provided.

**§2 Related Work:**
- The characterization that most video-based methods are "many-to-one" ignores that many-to-one methods can estimate all frames via sliding windows with temporal overlap. The efficiency argument against many-to-one is not substantiated.
- Table 1 positions SoloPose as uniquely checking all boxes, but KTPFormer is listed as many-to-many with heatmap — the distinction is not clearly explained.

**§3 AugMotion:**
- Equation 1 computes $M_s$ and $M_{sp}$ as average distances, but these averages are over *all frames across all datasets*. This collapses body proportion variation and means the universal coordinate system is a compromise fit for nobody.
- The choice of reference point coordinates (-1,0,3), (1,0,3), (0,0,0.5) appears arbitrary and is not derived from the computed ratios $M_s/M_{sp}$.
- No discussion of how datasets with different skeleton definitions (different numbers/types of keypoints) are harmonized.

**§4 SoloPose:**
- The spatial feature map size is stated as $1 \times 200 \times 192$ per frame. The CLIP model being used is unspecified (ViT-B/32? ViT-L/14?). This matters enormously for compute cost and feature quality.
- The temporal transformer uses Swin blocks with "3D relative position embedding" — this is under-specified. What are the 3D relative distances? How is the bias matrix parameterized?
- N=30 frames is chosen "based on the experiments" but no experiment comparing sequence lengths is shown.
- The heatmap head uses "3 convolutional neural networks" — what does this mean? Three conv layers? Three separate CNNs? The architecture is unclear.
- The 3D heatmap volume dimensions $w \times h \times d$ are never specified. This determines the spatial resolution and quantization error of the output.

**§5 Experiments:**
- No standard deviations or statistical significance reported.
- All baselines use N=243 frames while SoloPose uses N=30. This is a confound: more input frames generally help temporal models. The paper does not discuss this discrepancy.
- The "SoloPose only trained on Human3.6M" ablation (47.9 MPJPE on H7.1M, 38.9 on H3.6M) actually shows SoloPose's architecture underperforms relative to SOTA when given the same training data. The paper spins this as demonstrating the value of data augmentation, but it simultaneously undermines the architectural contribution claims.
- No computational cost comparison (FLOPs, parameters, training time, inference speed) is provided anywhere, despite the paper's emphasis on "efficiency" and "cost-efficiency."

**§6 Conclusion:**
- Claims "improved performance over existing SOTA models" without acknowledging this is driven by training data, not architecture.

